    "text": "To solve the equation \\((2^x - 2)(5 \\cdot 2^x + 10) = 3 \\cdot 4^x + 12\\), we start by expressing \\(4^x\\) as \\((2^2)^x = (2^x)^2\\). Let's set \\(y = 2^x\\). The equation then becomes:\n\n\\[\n(y - 2)(5y + 10) = 3y^2 + 12\n\\]\n\nFirst, we expand the left-hand side:\n\n\\[\n(y - 2)(5y + 10) = y \\cdot 5y + y \\cdot 10 - 2 \\cdot 5y - 2 \\cdot 10 = 5y^2 + 10y - 10y - 20 = 5y^2 - 20\n\\]\n\nSo the equation simplifies to:\n\n\\[\n5y^2 - 20 = 3y^2 + 12\n\\]\n\nNext, we move all terms to one side of the equation:\n\n\\[\n5y^2 - 20 - 3y^2 - 12 = 0\n\\]\n\nCombine like terms:\n\n\\[\n2y^2 - 32 = 0\n\\]\n\nAdd 32 to both sides:\n\n\\[\n2y^2 = 32\n\\]\n\nDivide both sides by 2:\n\n\\[\ny^2 = 16\n\\]\n\nTake the square root of both sides:\n\n\\[\ny = 4 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad y = -4\n\\]\n\nSince \\(y = 2^x\\) and \\(2^x\\) is always positive, we discard \\(y = -4\\). Thus, we have:\n\n\\[\ny = 4\n\\]\n\nRecall that \\(y = 2^x\\), so:\n\n\\[\n2^x = 4\n\\]\n\nWe know that \\(4\\) can be written as \\(2^2\\), so:\n\n\\[\n2^x = 2^2\n\\]\n\nSince the bases are the same, we can equate the exponents:\n\n\\[\nx = 2\n\\]\n\nThus, the value of \\(x\\) is \\(\\boxed{2}\\).",
    "timing_scope": "native prompt render, tokenize, transfer, CUDA-synchronized generate, decode; excludes loading/scoring",
